# RBR Hospital — nightly backup.
#
# One entry, calling deploy/hospital-backup.sh. Everything it does and every
# setting it takes is documented in the header of that script; read it before
# changing anything here.
#
# Without this there is no backup schedule at all — which is not a silent
# failure like the queue worker or the scheduler, it is no failure at all until
# the day the disk goes, and then it is the only one that matters. A MySQL
# schema of appointments and patients, and a private disk of reports and
# prescriptions, both of which exist in exactly one place until this runs.
#
# Install:
#   sudo install -m 750 deploy/hospital-backup.sh /usr/local/sbin/hospital-backup
#   sudo install -d -m 700 /var/backups/hospital
#   sudo cp deploy/hospital-backup.cron /etc/cron.d/hospital-backup
#   sudo chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/hospital-backup
#   sudo systemctl restart cron
#
# Check it:
#   sudo /usr/local/sbin/hospital-backup          # take one now, by hand
#   ls -l /var/backups/hospital                   # a directory per run
#   tail /var/log/hospital-backup.log
#   grep hospital-backup /var/log/syslog          # that cron is firing at all
#
# And twice a year, restore one — into a scratch database, not the live one:
#   deploy/hospital-restore.sh --db-only --database hospital_restore_check \
#       /var/backups/hospital/hospital-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS
#   mysql -e 'DROP DATABASE hospital_restore_check'
# A backup nobody has restored is a hope. The script counts the rows back and
# compares them with the manifest, so the rehearsal is a minute's work.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=rashedul@rightbrainsolution.com

# Off-site. Until this is set, every backup sits on the same disk as the
# database it is protecting, and protects against precisely one thing:
# somebody deleting rows. Not the disk failing, which is the likelier of the
# two. Any rsync destination — another host, a mounted volume, a NAS.
#HOSPITAL_BACKUP_REMOTE=backups@10.0.0.9:/srv/backups/hospital/

# 02:30. After the evening's bookings and the 18:00 reminder run, and before
# the 03:00 prune in routes/console.php — so a backup taken tonight still holds
# the notification log rows that prune is about to age out.
#
# The box runs on Asia/Dhaka, the same clock the application stores its
# timestamps in, so this is 02:30 local with nothing to convert.
30 2 * * * root /usr/local/sbin/hospital-backup >> /var/log/hospital-backup.log 2>&1
